<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-intro" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Starting with Data</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We're starting this chapter, and the book, with an exercise.
      That's probably not what you expect from a first chapter, but there's a
      good reason for it.
      We haven't introduced any techniques yet, so you'll be working purely from
      what you already know about data, which might be a lot, or might be very
      little.
      Let's just try it and see what we can do with a dataset by looking at it
      carefully and without instruction.
      The rest of the chapter then introduces the structure of the book, and
      comes back to the exercise to show how it connects to the chapters that
      follow.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-first-exploration">
    <title>A First Exploration</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Most of the datasets in this book come from the <c>statsdata</c> R
        package.
        You can install it once in R by:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    # install.packages("pak")
    pak::pak("PreTeXtBooks/statsdata")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Then load it at the start of your R session:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    library(statsdata)
    data(kenya_flamingos)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The flamingo dataset we use in this chapter is then available from the
        package:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    head(kenya_flamingos)
    #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 8
    #&gt;   lake    species           bill_length_mm bill_depth_mm
    #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                      &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Bogoria Lesser Flamingo             101.          25.9
    #&gt; 2 Nakuru  Greater Flamingo            136.          22.5
    #&gt; ...
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        If you'd rather use other software, the raw CSV files are available to
        download from the package repository at
        <url href="https://github.com/PreTeXtBooks/statsdata/tree/main/data-raw">github.com/PreTeXtBooks/statsdata</url>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-the-data">
      <title>The dataset</title>

      <p>
        While most of the datasets in this book are real, this first one is
        simulated.
        It is grounded in flamingo populations at three lakes in Kenya's Rift
        Valley - Lake Bogoria, Lake Elementaita, and Lake Nakuru.
        The dataset <c>kenya_flamingos</c>, available through the
        <c>statsdata</c> R package, has one row per bird, with the columns
        described in <xref ref="table-flamingo-columns"/>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-flamingo-columns">
        <title>Columns in <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c></title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
          <col halign="left" width="75%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Column</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>lake</c></cell>
            <cell>Lake at which the bird was recorded</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>species</c></cell>
            <cell>Greater Flamingo or Lesser Flamingo</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>bill_length_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Bill length, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>bill_depth_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Bill depth, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>leg_length_mm</c></cell>
            <cell>Leg length, in millimetres</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>body_mass_g</c></cell>
            <cell>Body mass, in grams</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>sex</c></cell>
            <cell>Recorded sex of the bird</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell><c>year</c></cell>
            <cell>Year of measurement</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The dataset is small enough to look at in a spreadsheet, in R, or
        printed out on paper.
        Which one you pick doesn't matter for this exercise, it's entirely up to
        you.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-first-exploration">
      <title>Thirty minutes with the data</title>

      <p>
        Spend thirty minutes with the flamingo dataset and write down three
        things you notice.
        They can be anything you can get from the data, as simple or as
        complicated as you like: the number of rows, the years the simulated
        data covers, the number of missing values, a graph, a summary, how many
        birds are at each lake or of each species, or anything else.
        The three things aren't important, and they don't even need to be right!
        They just need to be three things you can see in the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        Write your three observations down before you read the discussion that
        follows.
        The discussion lists what other readers noticed, and reading it first
        takes away the whole point of the exercise.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-results-ish">
    <title>What the Data Showed</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section is the other half of the exercise.
        You've spent thirty minutes with the flamingo data and written down
        three things, so now you can compare what you found with what other
        readers found.
        There's no correct list to check your answers against.
        What we want you to see is how much a dataset gives up to someone who
        simply looks at it, and how almost every observation raises a further
        question that looking alone can't settle.
        Those further questions are what the rest of the book is about.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="what-did-others-find">
      <title>What did others find?</title>

      <p>
        You can let us know on the Discussion GitHub page what you found.
        Here are some examples.
        If you didn't find any of these, that's fine.
        This book is about how to do all of these explorations.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              There are eight columns and 344 rows in the data.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
data("kenya_flamingos")
dim(kenya_flamingos)
View(kenya_flamingos)
              </code>
            </program>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Bill depth ranges from 18.10 mm to 33.90 mm, and there are four
              missing values.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
summary(kenya_flamingos$bill_depth_mm)
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              A simple summary like this tells us quite a lot: the smallest and
              largest values, some values in between, the mean, and how many
              values are missing.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              There are two species in the data, Greater Flamingos and Lesser
              Flamingos, and Lesser Flamingos outnumber Greater Flamingos by a
              substantial margin.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$species)
              </code>
            </program>
            <pre>
Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
             66              278
            </pre>
            <p>
              These counts tell us which birds were measured, not which birds
              were there.
              Maybe there just happened to be more Lesser Flamingos around on
              the day the data was collected.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Greater Flamingos are heavier than Lesser Flamingos, by roughly a
              factor of two.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
library(dplyr)

kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
  group_by(species) %&gt;%
  summarise(mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
              </code>
            </program>
            <pre>
# A tibble: 2 x 2
  species           mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
  &lt;chr&gt;                              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Greater Flamingo                    3120
2 Lesser Flamingo                     1689
            </pre>
            <p>
              This difference is big enough that anything we say about
              <q>flamingos</q> in general will depend heavily on the mix of
              species in the data.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              There are 170 females and 167 males in the data, and seven rows
              with no recorded sex.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$sex)
              </code>
            </program>
            <pre>
female   male
    170    167
            </pre>
            <p>
              Flamingos could be hard to sex from how they look, so this is a
              plausible limitation of the data collection rather than a mistake.
              But any comparison between male and female birds will quietly drop
              those rows unless we deal with the missing values on purpose.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              There's 124 measurements from Bogoria, 68 from Elementaita, and
              152 from Nakuru.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
table(kenya_flamingos$lake)
              </code>
            </program>
            <pre>
Bogoria Elementaita      Nakuru
    124          68         152
            </pre>
            <p>
              This might reflect real differences in the flamingo populations,
              or differences in how much survey effort went into each lake, or
              something else entirely.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              As bill length increases, body mass tends to increase as well.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
plot(kenya_flamingos$bill_length_mm,
     kenya_flamingos$body_mass_g)
              </code>
            </program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-plot1">
              <caption>
                Bill length against body mass for Kenyan flamingos.
              </caption>

              <image source="images/ch1_plot1.jpeg" width="100%">
                <description>
                  <p>
                    A scatter plot of bill length against body mass for Kenyan
                    flamingos.
                    There is a positive trend, and two clear groups.
                  </p>
                </description>
              </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
              We could make the same plot with <c>ggplot2</c>, a plotting tool
              covered in <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
library(ggplot2)

ggplot(kenya_flamingos,
       aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_point()
              </code>
            </program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-plot2">
              <caption>
                Bill length against body mass for Kenyan flamingos using
                <c>ggplot</c>.
              </caption>

              <image source="images/ch1_plot2.png" width="100%">
                <description>
                  <p>
                    A scatter plot of bill length against body mass for Kenyan
                    flamingos.
                    There is a positive trend, and two clear groups.
                  </p>
                </description>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Birds with longer bills tend to be heavier.
              But, also, Greater Flamingos tend to have both a higher body mass
              and a longer bill than Lesser Flamingos.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
ggplot(kenya_flamingos,
       aes(x = bill_length_mm,
           y = body_mass_g,
           colour = species)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm")
              </code>
            </program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-plot3">
              <caption>
                Bill length against body mass for Kenyan flamingos using
                <c>ggplot</c>, with each group coloured and a line of best fit.
              </caption>

              <image source="images/ch1_plot3.png" width="100%">
                <description>
                  <p>
                    A scatter plot of bill length against body mass for Kenyan
                    flamingos.
                    There is a positive trend, and two clear groups.
                    Each group is coloured, with a line of best fit for each
                    group.
                  </p>
                </description>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        You might have spotted things that aren't mentioned here at all.
        For example:
        <ul>
          <li>Do males and females differ in body mass?</li>
          <li>Does bill length differ between the two species?</li>
          <li>Are the flamingos at different lakes different sizes?</li>
          <li>Is there a relationship between bill depth and bill length?</li>
          <li>Are there any unusually large or small flamingos?</li>
          <li>
            Are missing values concentrated in particular variables or groups?
          </li>

          <li>Does the balance of species differ between the three lakes?</li>
          <li>
            Does the relationship between bill length and body mass look the
            same for both species?
          </li>

          <li>Can you identify individual observations that look unusual?</li>
          <li>What questions can't this dataset answer?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't need to have found any of these in particular.
        The point of the activity was to explore the data and see what questions
        you could ask of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are just some of the things people noticed when they explored the
        same data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-what-the-exercise-covered">
      <title>What the exercise covered</title>

      <p>
        The things you can notice in this dataset line up closely with the
        topics of the chapters that follow.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ways to explore the data, including the techniques in the R code in
        <xref ref="what-did-others-find"/>, are covered in
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, with more advanced visualisations in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
        The missing values in the <c>sex</c> column, and the question of what to
        do about them, are the subject of
        <xref ref="ch-handling-missing-values"/>, with other data cleaning
        methods covered in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
        Comparing species, lakes or years means treating those variables as
        categories, which is what <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/> is about.
        The bill length and body mass relationship, which holds across the whole
        dataset but not within each species, is an example of the problem we
        look at in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
        How spread out a measurement is, rather than just what its average value
        is, is the subject of <xref ref="ch-variability"/>, and accounting for
        those differences is the subject of
        <xref ref="ch-accounting-variability"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-about-this-book">
    <title>About This Book</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This book is an introduction to working with data.
        It's about the work that has to happen before any statistical result can
        be trusted: getting data into a usable shape, understanding how it was
        collected, deciding what to do about the parts that are missing or
        miscoded, and spotting when a pattern is an artefact of how the data is
        structured rather than something real about the world.
      </p>

      <p>
        We've written it for people who are starting to work with real data:
        students meeting statistics for the first time in a computational
        setting, and researchers or practitioners who have data to analyse and
        want to do it well.
        The examples come from real situations, many of them from Africa, and
        many of them cases where the data was handled badly and the consequences
        were serious.
      </p>

      <p>
        We don't assume you've used R before, and we don't use any mathematics
        beyond arithmetic.
        What we do assume is that you're willing to run code, look at the
        output, and ask whether it means what it looks like it means.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-structure">
      <title>Structure</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> begins with data that has structure:
        individuals belonging to groups, where the group shapes both an
        individual's outcome and how common that individual is in the data.
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> covers exploring data by summarising,
        tabulating, and plotting it to know what you're holding.
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> deals with data cleaning:
        inconsistent categories, implausible values, and entries that were
        recorded under several different names.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> takes up the problem
        of visualising multiple columns at once, where a table holds the answer
        but can't display it.
        <xref ref="ch-data-shapes"/> is about the arrangement of the data
        itself, and how the same information laid out differently makes some
        questions easier to answer and some answers easier to visualise.
        <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/> covers factors, including how R stores
        them, how their levels are ordered, and how much rests on decisions
        about which categories exist at all.
        <xref ref="ch-handling-missing-values"/> asks why values are missing,
        and shows that the answer changes how to handle them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The last two chapters turn from the value of a measurement to its
        spread.
        <xref ref="ch-variability"/> introduces variability, why it matters, and
        how to measure it.
        <xref ref="ch-accounting-variability"/> then looks at accounting for
        that variability.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-chapter-format">
      <title>Chapter format</title>

      <p>
        Most chapters follow the same structure.
        Each one opens with a case study to introduce the problem, usually a
        real situation where the way the data was handled really mattered.
        Following this is a <term>STACK question</term> to give you a chance to
        try the idea for yourself before we go any further.
        The chapter then develops the ideas using data, with R code alongside,
        to illustrate how to deal with the problem, step by step, using real
        data.
        Exercises come at the end of each chapter, to let you practise, and
        check that it has stuck.
        Some chapters also include interactive questions at the end too.
        These are often mastery, which means that each time they are attempted
        there are a new set of values in the question.
        As a result, they can be completed as many times as you like.
      </p>

      <p>
        To follow the code you'll need R, available from
        <url href="https://cran.r-project.org/">cran.r-project.org</url>, and
        we'd recommend installing RStudio too, from
        <url href="https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop/">posit.co</url>,
        which gives you a more comfortable place to write and run R code.
        Both are free.
      </p>

      <p>
        The datasets used in this book are distributed in the <c>statsdata</c>
        package, which installs from GitHub rather than from CRAN:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
install.packages("pak") # only needed once

pak::pak("PreTeXtBooks/statsdata")
library(statsdata)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Each dataset is then available by name, as with
        <c>data(kenya_flamingos)</c> earlier in this chapter.
        A few examples use data downloaded straight from an external source
        instead.
        Where that happens, the chapter gives you the link and the steps needed
        to read the file into R.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>